I implemented `satisfies` and `maxSatisfying` in `src/semver.ts` as a port of how node-semver v7 parses and checks ranges, with no new dependencies. `bun test` passes: 3 of 3 tests, 0 failures.

I also ran 37 extra hand-picked cases in a scratch script. All passed, and I did not add them to the repo. They covered:
- hyphen ranges with partial versions;
- `~0.2.3`, `^0.0.3`, `^0.x` and `^0.0.x`;
- prereleases, including the rule that they only match when a comparator has a prerelease on the same `major.minor.patch`;
- build metadata, which is ignored;
- `||`, operators written with a space (`>= 1.2.3`, `~ 1.2`), and an empty range (it matches everything, as in node-semver);
- invalid versions and ranges: `satisfies` returns `false`, `maxSatisfying` returns `null`.

My expected results came from node-semver's rules as I know them, not from running the real package next to my code. A rare case could still differ from npm `semver`. I also didn't run a TypeScript type check: there is no `tsconfig`, and `bun test` doesn't check types.
